WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Do you remember any hurricanes or floods in the Ninth Ward while you were living there?

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.740
Betsy happened a year to the day that my daddy died.

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.480
He died in September of '64, September 8, 1964.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.640
And I think Betsy was September 9, 1965.

5
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.520
I remember . . . Looking back as an adult,

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:27.160
I can just imagine the fear of my mother having us six [children] in an old shotgun house.

7
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.780
And I can remember the house kind of moving a little bit during the night.

8
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.320
Aunt Shirley slept through the whole hurricane.

9
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.040
And my mother was up all night with the six kids.

10
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.880
We had a cinderblock fence alongside of the back part of our house.

11
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:51.880
All of a sudden there was a big gigantic boom and the house just kind of went, you know, shook.

12
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.340
I can remember the hurricane really shaking, shaking.

13
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.520
Everything was scary, scary, scary.

14
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.860
Then when we got to the eye, everything was silent and still, I can remember that.

15
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.940
That's when my mother told my brother to look out and see what happened.

16
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.160
She's holding him and he looked out the side door and he said, "Oh the wall fell."

17
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.740
And my mother thought it was the wall of the house and she freaked.

18
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:20.440
But it was the cinder block wall just kind of fell and fell right onto our house.

19
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.200
And like I say, Aunt Shirley slept the whole time.

20
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:32.660
Well, when Aunt Shirley woke up the next morning, not even giving any thought to my mother [laughs] and the hurricane, whatever.

21
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:37.040
She's washing her dishes and her kitchen window looked out.

22
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:44.500
She's washing her dishes and all of a sudden she sees like this tidal wave coming toward her.

23
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.120
She said by the time she turned around and said, "Oliver," to her husband, "there's water coming," there was three feet of water in her house.

24
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.300
So her and her six kids and her husband, found a boat,

25
00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:03.260
found a way to get to us and came and we were high and dry because the closer you are to the levee,

26
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.100
the higher the ground is.

27
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.140
The water did not stay that long.

28
00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:13.260
It came . . . It wasn't like Katrina with us, it didn't sit here for two weeks like it did here.

29
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.540
It came and then it pretty much left within a day or so.

30
00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.940
So we were high and dry, we made it through the hurricane and it was very scary.

31
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:36.220
But my husband who lived in the upper Ninth Ward, his garage which unfortunately had all his baby pictures,

32
00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:42.500
his mother stored everything in the wrong place [laughs], so he lost everything, all his baby pictures, everything.

33
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:53.460
And you know my Aunt Shirley you know none of her kids have their baby pictures because they lost everything, sort of like Katrina with us.

34
00:02:53.460 --> 00:03:02.540
But it [Betsy] was a scary . . . Yeah that was the only hurricane I . . . And we never had another hurricane until Katrina.

35
00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:10.720
That's what kills me when people say, "Oh, New Orleans is so under water, under sea level.

36
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.820
Why would you rebuild?"

37
00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.080
We had a hurricane forty years ago and we had another one [laughs].

38
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:22.140
Florida has hurricanes in one part or another of their state every year, you know.

39
00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:28.720
Of course, Camille hit a few years after that, but it didn't hit New Orleans, it hit Mississippi.

40
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:36.820
My husband was at St. Stanislaus when it hit there and when he walked the coast of Mississippi as a high schooler,

41
00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:42.540
he thought he'd never see anything as bad as what Camille was.

42
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.820
Every other house was down.

43
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.760
And when he went back for Katrina,

44
00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:53.840
because he was in law enforcement and he walked . . . He said not only every house is gone,

45
00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:00.180
every house for three blocks inland is gone, which Camille didn't do that.

46
00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:07.420
A lot of those historic plantations and everything survived Camille, but they didn't survive Katrina.

47
00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:13.860
I tell people all the time, New Orleans did not get hit by Katrina, Mississippi got hit by Katrina.

48
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.800
We got hit by stupidity, by lack of well-built levees.
